Image 1 is this young creature drawn in HiBit. It gives structure, proportion, pose and the place of every colour and marking only. It is never a rendering reference: its pixel steps and flat clusters are not the finish. Image 2 is the species reference: match its material, finish and light, not its shape, feet or wings. Image 3 is the colour key, as labels for where each pigment lies.

Paint the same creature in the rich Miniature Lives finish: smooth, rounded, softly modelled volumes, one key light from the top left, warm highlights, cool shadows, saturated playful colour, never dull. Limbs are living limbs, rounded and jointed, never boxes or unfinished 3D. Feet are planted with weight. Eyes keep their drawn size and read large, wet and lit. Put special care into the surface, which is the creature's beauty: paint the surface the species notes name so that it catches the light, every feather, hair or wing scale too fine to count but felt, bright and never dull.

Change only the rendering. Keep the silhouette, every part and its place, the proportions, the pose, the gaze, each pigment in its area, and only the markings the description names; add nothing.

Not: boxes, blocks, facets, planks, flat boards, clay or plastic sheen, photographic fur noise, glitter, invented markings, extra parts, scene, cast shadow, halo, text. Flat #f6f3ec ground; subject the same size and place as Image 1.

